The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
alg = __crypto_alg_lookup(name, type | test, mask | test);
if (!alg && test)
    alg = __crypto_alg_lookup(name, type, mask) ? ERR_PTR(-ELIBBAD) : NULL;
```
Reason:
The first statement attempts to look up a crypto algorithm with the tested flag set. If the algorithm is not found and the tested flag is set, the second statement erroneously checks for the algorithm again without considering if it is a "larval" algorithm. This leads to returning `-ELIBBAD` even for algorithms that are still in the process of being initialized (larval), causing allocation failures when multiple processes access encrypted files.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (alg && !crypto_is_larval(alg)) {
    crypto_mod_put(alg);
    alg = ERR_PTR(-ELIBBAD);
}
```
Reason:
These statements check if the algorithm found is not a larval algorithm before returning `-ELIBBAD`. This ensures that only non-larval algorithms, which have truly failed their tests, result in the error code, thus preventing the incorrect allocation failures when multiple processes access encrypted files simultaneously.

